Part 1: Architecture, Engineering, and Performance
1.1 Overall Architecture and Key Components
Warden is built as a layered, modular system with strict separation of concerns. The main components are:
•	Collectors – I/O heavy modules that read the machine via WMI, PowerShell, sockets, files, and psutil. They run on a thread pool and produce raw Observation objects.
•	ObservationStore – an in memory thread safe ring buffer that holds the latest readings (with 10 minute retention and a per source limit). It provides helpers like latest(), history(), and last_where().
•	Detectors – deterministic, stateless functions that examine the store and raise symptoms. They are pure logic, with no I/O, making them easily testable.
•	DetectorBank – manages the raise/clear state of symptoms, applying debouncing (require multiple consecutive samples) and root cause suppression (don’t report child symptoms if a parent is already active).
•	Reasoner – the decision engine that takes symptoms and produces a Diagnosis. It tries three brains in order: Cloud (Groq, optional), Local (Qwen2.5 1.5B via Ollama), and Rules (deterministic fallback).
•	Guardrail – validates the reasoner’s output: checks action ID is in the candidate set, grounds parameters against observed facts, rejects empty candidate symptoms, and drops invalid citations.
•	Executor – the only code that changes the machine. It runs the argv list with shell=False, re derives the command from the registry, checks admin rights, and requires an explicit approved_at timestamp.
•	Verifier – re runs relevant collectors after execution and evaluates a pre declared predicate, returning a tri state result (passed/failed/inconclusive).
•	Orchestrator (Agent) – manages the agent loop: tick, incident lifecycle, event bus, and coordination of all components. It offloads slow operations to threads/tasks so the event loop stays responsive.
•	EventBus – an append only event stream that broadcasts all state changes (telemetry, logs, incidents, etc.) to subscribers (WebSocket, session recorder).
The layers communicate through contracts (warden/contracts/) – frozen Pydantic schemas that import nothing else. This ensures a strict acyclic dependency graph and allows independent evolution of each layer.
Why contracts? They enforce type safety across the entire stack. The front end TypeScript types are generated from the backend’s OpenAPI spec, so any change to a contract causes a compile time error if the UI hasn’t been regenerated. They also serve as self documenting data structures with extra="forbid", catching any unknown fields (e.g., model hallucinations, hand edited fixtures).
Why split collectors and detectors? Collectors are I/O heavy; detectors are pure logic. This gives testability (detectors can be tested with hand built observations, no Windows required), performance (collectors run on a thread pool at different intervals), and separation of concerns (collectors own the how; detectors own the what).
Why does the orchestrator exist? It decouples control flow from the HTTP API. The orchestrator handles asynchronous diagnosis, event streaming, incident lifecycle, and concurrency control (only one action runs at a time). The API is lightweight and stateless.
1.2 Data Flow and State Management
The typical flow from a user action:
1.	User clicks “Look into this” → API calls Agent.check(domain).
2.	Agent retrieves active symptoms from the DetectorBank for that domain.
3.	For each symptom, it opens an incident and spawns an async diagnosis task.
4.	Diagnosis forces a context re read (collectors for event log, devices, processes) to get fresh data.
5.	It calls Reasoner.diagnose(), which tries cloud → local → rules.
6.	The reasoner builds a prompt with symptom facts, evidence, candidate actions, and exclusions.
7.	The guardrail validates the model’s response (or the rules produce a diagnosis directly).
8.	If actionable, the incident enters AWAITING_APPROVAL and the UI shows the proposal.
9.	On user approval, the executor runs the command with a timestamp, and the verifier checks the result.
10.	Depending on the verification outcome, the incident closes (RESOLVED), escalates (attempted_actions added, re diagnosed), or remains open if inconclusive.
State ownership: The Agent owns high level state (incidents, cooldowns, muted set, ObservationStore). The DetectorBank owns the active symptom set. The EventBus owns the event sequence. State is not persisted in the API layer – the API is stateless and queries the agent. This avoids global singletons and makes testing easier.
EventBus and synchronization: The UI connects via WebSocket. On connect, it fetches a full snapshot via GET /api/state. Then it streams events. If a sequence gap is detected (event.seq > lastSeq+1), the UI shows a resync button and fetches GET /api/events/recent?since=lastSeq to catch up. This ensures no events are lost even if the socket drops.
1.3 Windows Integration and Collectors
Warden uses CIM (WMI) and PowerShell cmdlets (e.g., Get-NetAdapter, Get-Service) because they are locale independent and return structured objects, unlike netsh output, which is localised and brittle. However, netsh is still used for Wi Fi details (SSID, signal, radio) because it’s the only readily available source for those – but its output is parsed tolerantly and cross validated against other sources.
Persistent PowerShell Bridge: Spawning powershell.exe costs ~500ms per invocation. With many probes running every few seconds, that overhead would be unacceptable. So Warden keeps a single PowerShell process alive and sends single line commands via stdin, reducing per probe latency to ~100ms. If a command times out, the process is killed and a fresh one is spawned – this ensures a hung probe never corrupts subsequent reads.
Temperature detection: Windows has no universal API for CPU temperature. Warden tries four providers in order: (1) LibreHardwareMonitor (needs DLL and admin), (2) OpenHardwareMonitor WMI, (3) ACPI thermal zones, (4) throttle inference. The inference uses PercentProcessorPerformance (from performance counters) and is always available. This chain ensures we always have at least a fallback.
Why PercentProcessorPerformance over CurrentClockSpeed? On the development machine, CurrentClockSpeed reported the rated base clock even under load – it never moved. PercentProcessorPerformance actually reflects the delivered clock percentage and changes under throttling, making it a reliable signal.
Collector intervals: Ranging from 2s (Wi Fi, CPU) to 120s (audit settings). The intervals are tuned to balance responsiveness and CPU load – only due collectors run each tick.
1.4 Performance and Concurrency
•	Threading: Collectors run on a ThreadPoolExecutor with 6 workers. Slow probes (like sys.audit) don’t block others.
•	Asynchronous execution: The agent loop is asyncio based. Slow operations (probes, inference, execution, verification) are offloaded to threads using asyncio.to_thread.
•	Inference concurrency: The local model runs in its own thread (via httpx async, but the actual inference is server side). The UI remains responsive because the event loop is not blocked.
•	State locking: The ObservationStore uses an RLock for thread safety. The agent uses _execution_lock to ensure only one action runs at a time.
•	Memory usage: The application itself is ~45 MB. The observation store is bounded (400 readings per source, 10 minute retention). The local model (Qwen2.5 1.5B) uses ~1 GB of RAM when resident. Total usage is under 1.2 GB.
Scalability: Adding a new diagnostic domain (e.g., GPU) requires only a new collector, a new detector (if needed), and updating domains.py and playbooks/__init__.py – at most 3–4 files. Because the system is modular and contract driven, no changes to the orchestrator or UI are necessary (the UI renders any domain from the API).
1.5 UI and API Layer
•	Front end: React + TypeScript + Vite + Tailwind. Components are designed to be dense and calm, reflecting the instrument like aesthetic. Key components: ProposalCard (reviewed actions) and ComposedCard (cloud written commands) are distinct components with different colours and wording – users can tell them apart at a glance.
•	State derivation: The UI’s state is derived from the WebSocket events via a reducer. No client side state is authoritative – everything comes from the backend.
•	Zoom for projectors: useZoom listens for Ctrl+/- and applies CSS zoom, reflowing text instead of blurring, making it legible from a distance.
•	Desktop shell: pywebview uses the system’s WebView2 (Edge Chromium), which is already installed on most Windows 10/11 machines. This keeps the bundle tiny (~10 MB) vs Electron’s ~70 MB.
•	API: FastAPI runs on 127.0.0.1 – bound to loopback, so it’s not reachable from the network. It serves static files from ui/dist and provides REST endpoints for state, incidents, settings, and demo. WebSocket for real time events.
1.6 Testing and Build
•	Testing philosophy: The system is heavily tested (413 tests) because it’s safety critical. Tests are platform independent – they use hand built observations and never touch Windows. This allows them to run on CI in milliseconds and ensures coverage of all logic (detectors, guardrails, proposals, executor preflight, verifier predicates, audit).
•	Key test strategies:
o	conftest.py provides fixtures for ObservationStore and common symptoms.
o	test_approval_gate.py inspects the signature of Executor.execute to ensure approved_at has no default – this is a compile time guarantee that the gate cannot be bypassed.
o	test_false_verification.py pins specific bugs (multi line netsh parsing, symptom cleared ≠ fixed) to prevent regression.
•	Build system: Two editions: standard (~46 MB, ships runtime but no weights) and offline (~967 MB, bundles weights). Build uses PyInstaller (onedir) and Inno Setup for per user installation (no admin required). The installer checks for WebView2 and warns if missing. The build scripts (scripts/) automate fetching the model, building the UI, and packaging.
